  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe cold front is passing through the Quad State currently and \nbringing some widespread showers and storms with it. The main \nconcern at this point would be isolated flash flooding with any \ntraining storms (though the line appears to be moving pretty good at \nthis time). Conditions should dry out later this morning behind the \nfront. Some wrap around precip is possible today and tomorrow as the \nupper and sfc lows slowly begin to push east of the CWA. There \nshouldn't be any severe concerns given that we're behind the front. \nHowever, with the colder air aloft and the proximity of the low, \ncan't rule out a few cold air funnels. With the added cloud cover \ntoday and tomorrow and the potential for rain, highs may struggle to \nreach 80 in some places and may approach record cold highs. \n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, models are in better agreement with the \nplacement of the low being more to our east. Therefore, precip \nchances have decreased to ~20% in the Pennyrile Monday, and \nsouthwest IN/Pennyrile on Tuesday. Most of the Quad State will stay \ndry though. Ridging aloft builds into the Midwest Monday and Tuesday \nwith the low still slowly meandering to the east. \n\nBy Wednesday temperatures are back into the upper 80s and pushing 90 \nagain. Highs are back into the lower 90s by Friday. Unfortunately \ndew points climb back up into the mid 70s (or higher, as they have \nbeen over achieving this summer) on Friday and Saturday. This means \nheat indices return to the 100-105 degree range Friday and Saturday. \nShould dew points over achieve, a Heat Advisory may be needed.",
